9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B

The residual light-dependent Cy5 signal with the second probe is still best explained by limited phenoxyl-radical formation. Although the phenolic OH is gone, the benzylic hydroxymethyl substituent can undergo slow sensitized oxidation by the thioxanthenone under 417 nm irradiation; the resulting resonance-stabilized phenoxyl radical is then able to trigger a low-efficiency fragmentation that releases the alkyne tag for subsequent click labeling. This pathway is far less favorable than direct phenoxyl generation from the original phenol, which accounts for the much weaker but still observable fluorescence difference versus the no-light control.  

C is unlikely because the ring-opened Michael acceptor would be generated (if at all) by a purely thermal or hydrolytic route and would not produce a light-versus-dark difference. A cannot be the source of the difference because the thioxanthenone concentration and irradiation conditions are identical in both probe experiments; if it were responsible we would not see such a dramatic drop in signal intensity upon changing only the aryl substituent. D and E are ruled out on the same grounds as before. The data therefore point to B as the species that still operates, albeit inefficiently, with the hydroxymethyl probe.}

21.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B

The second probe's residual light-dependent Cy5 signal (weaker than the phenol version but still clearly above the dark control) is best explained by limited phenoxyl-radical formation from the hydroxymethyl substituent. Under 417 nm sensitization by the thioxanthenone, the benzylic alcohol can undergo slow oxidation to a resonance-stabilized phenoxyl species that still permits low-efficiency alkyne-tagged fragment release and protein labeling. This accounts for the observable SDS-PAGE difference without requiring the original phenolic OH.  

By contrast, option C (the Michael-acceptor fragment) would be expected to produce labeling that is independent of light, yet the experiment shows a clear light-versus-dark difference even with the modified probe; that observation is inconsistent with a purely thermal ring-opening pathway. The other choices are either common to both conditions (A, E) or chemically implausible without a diazirine (D). The drop in intensity relative to the first probe simply reflects the lower efficiency of hydroxymethyl-to-phenoxyl oxidation, which fits all reported data.}

